      "text": "Starting with a theoretical fluorine input of 2.568 mg from PFOA, only about 2.35% of fluorine was recovered after 120 min, with 0.0256 mg as aqueous fluoride, 0.018 mg in residual PFOA, 0.0067 mg in transformation products, and 0.01 mg adsorbed on catalyst. TOC decreased by 27.1%, indicating partial mineralization. This demonstrates that although PFOA degraded significantly, most fluorine remained in organic or adsorbed forms, underscoring the challenge of complete defluorination during photocatalysis. 3.9. Mineralization and mechanistic investigation via quenching To further support the proposed degradation mechanism, we assessed the degree of PFAS mineralization by comparing the total organic carbon (TOC) removal to the degradation efficiencies of PFOA and PFOS. 120 min of visible-light irradiation by CS@g-C \u2083 N \u2084 , the TOC removal was 27.1% for PFOA and 23% for PFOS, while their respective HPLC-based degradation efficacies were 93% and 88%. This gap verifies the generation of partially oxidized fluorinated intermediate products and indicates incomplete mineralization that is in agreement with the established PFAS degradation studies. Although direct identification of intermediates by LC-MS was not performed, the evidence of partial removal of TOC indicates a step-by-"
